# expand_named Step 8 (S-860): recursive type alias triggers Type::Recursive wrapping.
# When expand_named encounters a self-referential alias, Arc::ptr_eq detects the cycle
# (Step 4) and returns a TypeVar sentinel. After full expansion (Step 8), it detects
# the sentinel in the expanded body and wraps the result in Type::Recursive{var, body}.
#
# At runtime, the evaluator does not inspect Type::Recursive — it evaluates the dict
# and constructor calls directly. This test verifies that a recursive type alias:
#   1. Can be declared without a runtime error (expand_named does not infinite-loop)
#   2. Its constructors can be constructed and matched
#
# The recursive self-reference `tail: List` triggers expand_named Step 4 cycle detection.
# The test expects Int(1) from matching on a constructed List.Some node.
#
# Note: the type checker may emit warnings about the self-referential annotation
# (e.g. unification warnings from resolving `tail: List` in the type body).
# Those warnings are expected and captured in the === warn section.
[
  List: [type Cons: [val: Int  tail: List]  Nil]
  node: [List.Cons val: 1  tail: List.Nil]
  result: [match node
    [case [let p] [List.Cons p] p.val]
    List.Nil:                   0]
]
result
=== out
Int(1)
